but first our top story this half-hour  Israel and the West Bank are on edge  this morning as the region braces for  historic change clashes turned deadly  Friday between Israeli forces and  Palestinians at the Gaza border and  tensions are likely to increase ahead of  Monday's controversial relocation of the  US Embassy from Tel Aviv to Jerusalem  Jerusalem is of a particular concern as  Israelis and Palestinians both claim it  as their capital Holly Williams has more  from Gaza this morning Holly good  morning good morning here in Gaza  yesterday thousands of Palestinians  protested along that fence about a  hundred and fifty yards behind me that  separates the tiny Gaza Strip where they  live from Israel we saw Palestinian  demonstrators burning tires hurling  rocks and trying to pull down sections  of Defense Israeli soldiers on the other  side fired tear gas canisters and  bullets they've been holding these  protests now for around six weeks and  more than 40 Palestinians have been  killed including one yesterday hundreds  more have been injured the fence cuts  off the Palestinians who live here in  the Gaza Strip from the outside world  because of a more than ten year long  blockade that has made this  poverty-stricken place even more  desperate the Palestinian protesters are  demanding the right to return to land  that their families fled in 1948 when  the State of Israel was founded there  are about two million people living here  in the Gaza Strip an areas smaller than  the city of Denver and the majority are  refugees and their descendants the  protests are expected to grow bigger  next week the 70th anniversary of the  founding of the State of Israel when the  US will move its embassy to Jerusalem  that's been condemned by Palestinians  who hope that East Jerusalem will one  day be their capital if they ever get  their own state jury Quran Anthony Holly  Williams in Gaza thank you
 

